 The core is runtime-neutral: no `node:*`, no `Buffer`, no `process.env`, no SQL. The
 stores are subpath exports, so a consumer that never touches Postgres never loads it.
+
+## Confirmed usage and idempotent acknowledgements
+
+Products that require every served result to have a confirmed ledger write can
+opt out of the historical soft-fuse availability policy. This changes storage
+failure handling, while `fuseSoftWarn` still allows usage above the quota.
+
+```ts
+import { DurableObjectUsageLedger } from '@chester-hill-solutions/api-billing/durable-object';
+
+const ledger = new DurableObjectUsageLedger(env.API_KEY_USAGE, {
+  failureMode: 'closed',
+  acknowledgementRetries: 2,
+  requestTimeoutMs: 5000,
+});
+const operationId = crypto.randomUUID(); // One trusted ID for this logical operation.
+const nowMs = Date.now();               // Keep its original UTC billing month.
+const outcome = await recordSuccessfulBillable(customer, config, {
+  ledger, operationId, nowMs, usageFailureMode: 'closed',
+});
+```
+
+`usageFailureMode: 'closed'` refuses any explicitly unavailable ledger result,
+including a fail-open result from an unlimited or soft-fuse ledger. Confirmed
+exhaustion returns `429/FUSE_EXCEEDED`. An unavailable or unacknowledged store
+returns `503/USAGE_UNAVAILABLE`, with `operationId`, `month` and `uncertain` when
+an operation ID was supplied. A store that throws also produces an unavailable
+outcome. A store must advertise `supportsOperationIds` before the policy passes
+it an operation ID; unsupported stores return 503 before consumption.
+
+The DO client checks receipt-protocol support with GET before sending an
+operation-aware POST. The DO atomically stores the counter and the operation's
+receipt in a storage transaction. A lost POST response is recovered by reading
+that receipt; an absent receipt permits another POST with the **same** ID.
+Retries are bounded (two additional attempts by default, at most four), and each
+DO request has a timeout. No POST without an operation ID is retried.
+
+Existing `customer:<customerId>` object names and `month:<YYYY-MM>` counter keys
+are preserved. Receipts use additional `operation:month:<YYYY-MM>:<operationId>`
+keys. Old clients still use their original GET/POST contract. New clients refuse
+an old DO's missing protocol acknowledgement before sending any POST, avoiding
+repeated increments during a mixed-version rollout.
+
+Operation IDs are scoped to customer and month. Reuse the same ID **and original
+billing month** when reconciling one operation. Use a different ID for different
+work; never accept arbitrary client IDs without binding them to the authenticated
+logical operation. A receipt preserves the original decision even if the fuse
+later changes. The InMemory adapter follows the same deduplication contract.
+
+To reconcile a 503, call `ledger.consumeMonthly(customerId, enforcedLimit,
+originalNowMs, { operationId })` directly after connectivity recovers. It returns
+an existing receipt, or atomically records the operation if no receipt exists.
+This operation does not rerun the product's retrieval work. An exhausted quota
+precheck must not prevent receipt reconciliation. Report any recovered Stripe
+copy using the same operation ID: its hashed Stripe event identifier is stable
+for the customer, month and operation. Stripe delivery remains best effort; this
+package does not provide an outbox or guarantee provider-side receipt retention.
+
+**A permanent partition cannot make the HTTP response and storage commit atomic.**
+An uncertain 503 can coexist with a committed unit. `uncertain: true` means a
+consumption was attempted but acknowledgement could not be recovered within the
+retry budget. It never promises that the counter was unchanged. Recovering the
+receipt can establish the outcome later; delivering the product result later
+requires a product-level result journal. Retrying the whole product request with
+a fresh operation ID is distinct work and may consume another unit. Receipt
+records are retained; any operator retention policy must preserve the required
+reconciliation window. Do not delete receipts or reset counters during rollback.
+
+### Postgres operation receipts
+
+Apply the additive `USAGE_OPERATIONS_DDL` from the `./postgres` export before
+using operation IDs with an existing database. `USAGE_COUNTERS_DDL` continues to
+create only the existing counter table. The runner must expose transactions on
+a single connection:
+
+```ts
+const runner = {
+  unsafe: (query, params) => sql.unsafe(query, params),
+  transaction: (work) => sql.begin((transaction) => work({
+    unsafe: (query, params) => transaction.unsafe(query, params),
+  })),
+};
+const ledger = new PostgresUsageLedger(runner);
+```
+
+The transaction locks the existing customer/month counter row, reads the receipt,
+then updates the count and inserts the receipt together. Failed transactions
+roll back both writes. A lost COMMIT acknowledgement is retried with the same
+ID, so a committed receipt prevents a second increment. Existing no-operation
+calls keep their single-statement path and share the same counter/row lock.
+`acknowledgementRetries` and `operationTable` are optional third-constructor
+options. The SQL driver's connection/query/transaction timeouts must bound each
+attempt; this adapter does not cancel database transactions itself.
